ur so it cannot change silently in either direction; a dated non-capability note in README-l4 §4 says why it is what it is, with no historical line rewritten. STRAIN 2 — THE FORGETTING RECORD MUST BIND: autopsy/GAPMAP.md §2's engine thesis ("recorded but never binding" — every engine autopsied writes the metadata that would make it correct and then never reads it where it counts) turned on OUR OWN engine at the place a field note found it true (2026-07-26, the atlas-None seam: a payload that reads as an assertion but does NOT invert sets atlas[key]=None, and when the budget then releases its episode the engine books it as a DEMOTION while read(t) abstains on it forever — 8 such writes at a 40-cell cap gave demotions 7, forgetting-record count 0, read(0) None, so the ledger said nothing was lost and the content was gone). RED FIRST, and the recorded failure is the trial's own message: "the engine books 7 demotions but regenerates 0 events". What the trial demands is truthful ACCOUNTING and not a particular policy — a non-invertible fold is either FORGOTTEN (the record gains its count and its mass, read(t) abstains consistently) or REFUSED demotion and held as an episode, or refused outright under §4.1.2 with t unspent and the state unchanged — and it is NEVER booked as demoted while unregenerable; every identity is asserted against the PARTITION read off the ordinary query interface (§7), never against the counters' own arithmetic, which is the difference between a ledger that closes and a ledger that is true. THE ENGINE FIX, one line: THE DEMOTION INVARIANT IS INVERTIBILITY. `atlas_after` is now the ONE reading of the atlas transition, computed once per write and threaded through _accept/_apply_schema, so the entry the atlas ends up carrying and the entry the write path demoted by cannot be two different answers; an assertion whose key does not invert answers Q1/Q2 exactly, is stored where the inherited Layer-3 forgetting law can see it, and if released is released 